// Generated by tools/build_content.py. Part 5: Normalization
export default {"lead": "<p>Normalization is the process of checking each relation for \"problem\" functional dependencies and splitting the relation, without losing information, until those dependencies are gone. Each normal form names one kind of problem and bans it.</p>", "secs": [{"id": "p5-why-normalize", "t": "Why normalize", "h": "<p>The objectives: a good description of the data, and a stable set of relations that</p><ul><li>is a faithful model of the enterprise,</li><li>is highly flexible,</li><li>reduces redundancy, which saves space and prevents inconsistent data, and</li><li>is free of update, insertion and deletion anomalies.</li></ul><p>The last two matter most. By the time an ER diagram has been mapped to tables, most normalization issues are already resolved; normalization is the check that confirms each relation is at the level you want.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-the-three-modification-anomalies", "t": "The three modification anomalies", "h": "<p>An <strong>anomaly</strong> is an inconsistent, incomplete or contradictory state of the database. The table structure itself allows it to happen.</p><p>The lecture's example joins CUSTOMERS and ORDERS into one denormalized relation: <code class=\"rel\">CUST_ORDER(<span class=\"pk\">order_id</span>, customer_id, email_address, full_name, order_status, order_date)</code>. Each row is one order, so a customer's email and name repeat on every order that customer places.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Anomaly</th><th>Definition</th><th>In CUST_ORDER</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Update anomaly</td><td>A value is changed in one place but its other copies are not</td><td>Jennifer Cooper has several orders. Her new email is typed into one row only. Now her email depends on which order you look at.</td></tr><tr><td>Insertion anomaly</td><td>You cannot insert a fact when it occurs, because other unrelated data is required first</td><td>A new customer signs up but has not ordered. The primary key order_id cannot be null, so the customer cannot be stored until an order exists.</td></tr><tr><td>Deletion anomaly</td><td>Deleting one fact unintentionally deletes another</td><td>Jennifer Cooper has one order and cancels it. Deleting the row also deletes the only copy of her name and email.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The cure is the original design: CUSTOMERS and ORDERS as separate relations linked by customer_id.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-functional-dependencies", "t": "Functional dependencies", "h": "<p>A <strong>functional dependency (FD)</strong> is a relationship between attributes. B is functionally dependent on A if each value of A is associated with exactly one value of B. Equivalently: if two rows have the same A value, they must have the same B value.</p><ul><li>Written A → B. Read \"A functionally determines B\" or \"B is functionally dependent on A\".</li><li>The left side is the <strong>determinant</strong>. The right side is the <strong>dependent</strong>.</li><li>A and B can each be one attribute or a set of attributes.</li><li>An FD is a many-to-one relationship from A to B. Many students can share one status, but one credits value gives one status.</li><li>An FD is about meaning, not about cause or calculation. BusID → BusDriverID does not mean a driver number can be computed from a bus number.</li><li>An FD is a permanent rule about the relation, not a pattern in today's rows. Sample data can suggest an FD and can disprove one, but only logic can confirm one.</li></ul><p>Example: Student_ID → Student_Name. One ID belongs to one student, who has one name at a given time.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind of FD</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trivial</td><td>The dependent is a subset of the determinant. Always true, never useful.</td><td>{A, B} → {A}</td></tr><tr><td>Non-minimal</td><td>The determinant contains an attribute that adds nothing. The lecture also treats these as trivial.</td><td>If customer_id → full_name, then {customer_id, order_status} → full_name is true but pointless</td></tr><tr><td>Full functional dependency</td><td>B depends on A and on no proper subset of A: every attribute in the determinant is needed</td><td>{line_id, supplier_id} → quantity_shipped</td></tr><tr><td>Candidate key</td><td>A minimal determinant that determines every other attribute in the relation</td><td>order_id → everything else</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Every relation has at least one FD, because it must have a candidate key to have a primary key. Those FDs are required and harmless. Normalization hunts for the other ones.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-the-vocabulary-of-problem-dependencies", "t": "The vocabulary of problem dependencies", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prime attribute</td><td>An attribute that is part of any candidate key, not only the primary key</td></tr><tr><td>Non-key attribute</td><td>An attribute that is not prime: it is in no candidate key</td></tr><tr><td>Partial dependency (PD)</td><td>A non-key attribute is determined by only part of a composite primary key</td></tr><tr><td>Transitive dependency (TD)</td><td>If A → B and B → C, then C is transitively dependent on A. In practice: a non-key attribute determines another non-key attribute.</td></tr><tr><td>Non-superkey dependency</td><td>Any FD whose determinant is not a superkey</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A common error is reading \"non-key\" as \"not in the primary key\". It means \"not in any candidate key\".</p>"}, {"id": "p5-the-normal-forms-at-a-glance", "t": "The normal forms at a glance", "h": "<p>Each form includes all the requirements of the one before it and adds a stricter rule.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Rule added</th><th>What it removes</th><th>How to fix a violation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1NF</td><td>It is a relation: every attribute is single-valued in every row</td><td>Multivalued attributes</td><td>Move the multivalued attribute to a new child relation</td></tr><tr><td>2NF</td><td>No partial dependencies</td><td>Non-key attributes that depend on part of a composite key</td><td>Move the partial dependency to its own relation</td></tr><tr><td>3NF</td><td>No transitive dependencies</td><td>Non-key attributes that depend on other non-key attributes</td><td>Move the transitive dependency to its own relation</td></tr><tr><td>BCNF</td><td>Every determinant is a superkey</td><td>The remaining non-superkey dependencies, the ones that involve prime attributes</td><td>Move the offending dependency to its own relation; a dependency may be lost</td></tr><tr><td>4NF</td><td>No multivalued dependencies</td><td>Independent multivalued facts stored together</td><td>One relation per independent fact</td></tr><tr><td>5NF and beyond</td><td>Even stricter</td><td>Join dependencies</td><td>Generally not practical; research and special cases only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Which form to aim for:</p><ul><li>Operational, transaction-processing (OLTP) databases should usually be in <strong>3NF or BCNF</strong>.</li><li>Decision-oriented (OLAP) databases should be in <strong>at least 1NF</strong>, and usually should not be highly normalized.</li><li>4NF is seen in practice, but fewer organizations mandate it than 3NF or BCNF.</li><li>Say \"at least 2NF\" until you have checked for the next form: a relation may already satisfy a higher one.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p5-first-normal-form-1nf", "t": "First normal form (1NF)", "h": "<p><strong>Definition.</strong> A table is in 1NF, and is therefore a relation, if and only if every attribute is single-valued for each tuple and it meets all the other requirements of a relation. Each cell holds at most one value. Domains are atomic: no sets, lists, repeating fields or groups. A null is allowed, because null is the absence of a value.</p><p>Example. Suppose NSF wants to store several email addresses per customer. Then <code class=\"rel\">CUSTOMERS(<span class=\"pk\">customer_id</span>, email_address, full_name)</code> has a multivalued attribute and is not in 1NF. Three ways to fix it:</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What you do</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flattening</td><td>Add email_address to the primary key: (customer_id, email_address). One row per address, with full_name repeated.</td><td>Gets to 1NF but creates a partial dependency, so it trades one problem for a 2NF problem. Acceptable only for 1NF-only uses such as read-only or temporary tables.</td></tr><tr><td>Array of attributes</td><td>Create email_address_1, email_address_2, and so on</td><td>Gets to 1NF, but the maximum count is arbitrary, the later columns fill with nulls, and every query must check every column. Rarely preferred.</td></tr><tr><td>Move the multivalued attribute (preferred)</td><td>Create a new child relation holding the original primary key plus the multivalued attribute</td><td>Almost always the right answer</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The preferred solution:</p><ul><li><code class=\"rel\">CUSTOMERS(<span class=\"pk\">customer_id</span>, full_name)</code></li><li><code class=\"rel\">CUST_EMAILADDRESS(<span class=\"pk fk\">customer_id</span>, <span class=\"pk\">email_address</span>)</code></li></ul><p>In the new relation customer_id alone is not unique, so email_address joins it in the primary key. customer_id is also a foreign key back to CUSTOMERS. That foreign key is what lets the two relations be joined again, so nothing is lost. The new relation is an ID-dependent (weak) child.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-second-normal-form-2nf", "t": "Second normal form (2NF)", "h": "<p><strong>Definition.</strong> A relation is in 2NF if it is in 1NF and every non-key attribute is fully functionally dependent on the key. No non-key attribute depends on just part of the key.</p><p><strong>Shortcut.</strong> A partial dependency needs a composite primary key. If a relation is in 1NF and its primary key is a single attribute, it is automatically in 2NF.</p><p>Example (lecture). Add supplier_name to SHIPMENTS:</p><p><code class=\"rel\">SHIPMENTS(<span class=\"pk\">line_id</span>, <span class=\"pk\">supplier_id</span>, quantity_shipped, shipper, tracking_num, shipdate, supplier_name)</code></p><ul><li>{line_id, supplier_id} → {quantity_shipped, shipper, tracking_num, shipdate, supplier_name}. This is the candidate key and primary key.</li><li>{supplier_id} → {supplier_name}. supplier_id is only part of the key, so this is a partial dependency. The relation is in 1NF but not 2NF.</li></ul><p>Fix: move the partial dependency out and leave its determinant behind. The examples in this part mark only the foreign key created by the step being shown.</p><ul><li><code class=\"rel\">SHIPMENTS(<span class=\"pk\">line_id</span>, <span class=\"pk fk\">supplier_id</span>, quantity_shipped, shipper, tracking_num, shipdate)</code></li><li><code class=\"rel\">SUPPLIERS(<span class=\"pk\">supplier_id</span>, supplier_name)</code></li></ul><p>supplier_id stays in SHIPMENTS as part of its primary key and becomes a foreign key to SUPPLIERS. Joining the two relations on supplier_id recreates the original.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-third-normal-form-3nf", "t": "Third normal form (3NF)", "h": "<p><strong>Definition.</strong> A relation is in 3NF if it is in 2NF and has no transitive dependencies. The textbook's formal version: whenever a non-trivial FD X → A exists, either X is a superkey or A is a member of some candidate key. In plain words, every non-key attribute depends on the whole key and on nothing else.</p><p>The lecture's version differs slightly from the textbook's: it rules out any dependency that touches a prime attribute on either side. For this class, apply the lecture's two tests before you call something a transitive dependency:</p><ol><li><strong>No prime attributes are involved.</strong> If the determinant or the dependent contains any attribute from any candidate key, it is not a transitive dependency.</li><li><strong>The logical test.</strong> You must be able to argue that the dependent attribute is logically more dependent on the non-key determinant than on any candidate key.</li></ol><p>Prime-attribute drill from the lecture: if {A, B}, {C} and {F} are the candidate keys of R, then A, B, C and F are prime.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dependency</th><th>Can it be transitive?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>{A} → {D}</td><td>No</td><td>A is prime</td></tr><tr><td>{F, G, H, I} → {B}</td><td>No</td><td>F and B are prime</td></tr><tr><td>{C, H} → {G}</td><td>No</td><td>C is prime</td></tr><tr><td>{G} → {F}</td><td>No</td><td>F is prime</td></tr><tr><td>{D} → {E}</td><td>Yes</td><td>No prime attributes</td></tr><tr><td>{D, G} → {E}</td><td>Yes</td><td>No prime attributes</td></tr><tr><td>{H} → {D, E, G}</td><td>Yes</td><td>No prime attributes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Example (lecture). Add award_points to ORDER_ITEMS, where points are set by which price range the unit price falls in:</p><p><code class=\"rel\">ORDER_ITEMS(<span class=\"pk\">line_id</span>, order_id, line_item_id, product_id, award_points, unit_price, quantity)</code></p><ul><li>{line_id} → everything else. Candidate key (a surrogate) and the primary key.</li><li>{order_id, line_item_id} → everything else. Candidate key: an order has only one third line.</li><li>{order_id, product_id} → everything else. Candidate key: a product appears once per order; more units change quantity.</li><li>{unit_price} → {award_points}. Neither attribute is in any candidate key, and award_points is derived directly from unit_price, so it passes both tests. This is a transitive dependency.</li></ul><p>The primary key is a single attribute, so there are no partial dependencies: the relation is in 2NF but not 3NF.</p><p>Fix:</p><ul><li><code class=\"rel\">ORDER_ITEMS(<span class=\"pk\">line_id</span>, order_id, line_item_id, product_id, <span class=\"fk\">unit_price</span>, quantity)</code></li><li><code class=\"rel\">PRICE_AWARDS(<span class=\"pk\">unit_price</span>, award_points)</code></li></ul>"}, {"id": "p5-boyce-codd-normal-form-bcnf", "t": "Boyce-Codd normal form (BCNF)", "h": "<p><strong>Definition.</strong> A relation is in BCNF if, whenever a non-trivial FD X → A exists, X is a superkey. Every determinant must be a superkey.</p><p>BCNF is stricter than 3NF. 3NF lets a dependency stay if its dependent is a prime attribute; BCNF does not. If a relation has only one candidate key, 3NF and BCNF are the same thing. They differ only when there are multiple composite candidate keys that overlap, which is rare in practice but important when it happens.</p><p>Example (lecture and textbook). <code class=\"rel\">NEWFAC(<span class=\"pk\">fac_name</span>, <span class=\"pk\">dept</span>, office, rank, date_hired)</code>. Assume faculty names are unique within a department, each faculty member has one office, and each office belongs to one department.</p><ul><li>{fac_name, dept} → {office, rank, date_hired}. Candidate key, chosen as primary key.</li><li>{fac_name, office} → {dept, rank, date_hired}. Candidate key.</li><li>{office} → {dept}. office is not a superkey.</li></ul><p>Prime attributes: fac_name, dept, office. The relation is in 3NF, because office → dept has a prime dependent and so is not a transitive dependency. It is not in BCNF, because office is a determinant that is not a superkey.</p><p>Fix:</p><ul><li><code class=\"rel\">NEWFAC(<span class=\"pk\">fac_name</span>, <span class=\"pk fk\">office</span>, rank, date_hired)</code></li><li><code class=\"rel\">DEPTOFFICE(<span class=\"pk\">office</span>, dept)</code></li></ul><p>Removing dept destroyed the original primary key, so the remaining candidate key {fac_name, office} is promoted to primary key. If no candidate key survives, add a surrogate key.</p><p>The cost: {fac_name, dept} → {office, rank, date_hired} can no longer be seen or enforced, because fac_name and dept now live in different relations. That is what \"BCNF does not preserve all dependencies\" means.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Choose</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>BCNF</td><td>The goal is to remove the most possible sources of anomalies, and the lost dependency does not matter</td></tr><tr><td>3NF</td><td>Every dependency must be preserved, for example because users will search by the candidate key that BCNF would split</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The choice can be made relation by relation.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-fourth-normal-form-4nf", "t": "Fourth normal form (4NF)", "h": "<p>4NF deals with <strong>multivalued dependencies</strong>. A multivalued dependency exists when one attribute has two other attributes associated with it that are independent of each other and that can each take several values for a single value of the first. Every combination must then be stored: 2 values of one times 2 values of the other needs 4 rows, and the count grows fast.</p><p>The lecture lists 4NF as \"3NF plus no multivalued dependencies\"; most references define it on top of BCNF.</p><p>Fix: create one relation for each of the dependent attributes, each holding only that attribute and the determinant.</p><p>Illustration (constructed for this guide, not from the class files). An employee has several skills and speaks several languages, and the two facts are unrelated.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>emp</th><th>skill</th><th>language</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lee</td><td>SQL</td><td>English</td></tr><tr><td>Lee</td><td>SQL</td><td>Spanish</td></tr><tr><td>Lee</td><td>Python</td><td>English</td></tr><tr><td>Lee</td><td>Python</td><td>Spanish</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two skills times two languages gives four rows to say four separate facts. Adding one language means adding one row per skill. The 4NF design is <code class=\"rel\">EMP_SKILL(<span class=\"pk\">emp</span>, <span class=\"pk\">skill</span>)</code> and <code class=\"rel\">EMP_LANGUAGE(<span class=\"pk\">emp</span>, <span class=\"pk\">language</span>)</code>: two rows each.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-fifth-normal-form-and-beyond", "t": "Fifth normal form and beyond", "h": "<p>5NF deals with <strong>join dependencies</strong>, the third kind of dependency the textbook names. The lecture's position is that 5NF over-normalizes in virtually every situation and is used only in very special circumstances and research, so discard it for practical work. You may also see domain-key normal form (DKNF) mentioned in other references; it is not in the class material.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-lossless-decomposition", "t": "Lossless decomposition", "h": "<p><strong>Decomposition</strong> means taking a relation with a structural problem and dividing it into two or more smaller relations that do not have the problem. It is the technique behind every step from 1NF to BCNF.</p><p>A good decomposition has three properties:</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Attribute preservation</td><td>Every attribute ends up in some relation</td></tr><tr><td>Dependency preservation</td><td>Every FD of the original can still be found within a single relation. Always achievable through 3NF; not always in BCNF.</td></tr><tr><td>Lossless join</td><td>Joining the new relations gives back exactly the original relation, with no <strong>spurious tuples</strong> (false rows that were not in the original)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A two-way split is lossless when the attributes the two relations share are a superkey of one of them. Putting a determinant and its dependents in the new relation, and keeping the determinant in the original, guarantees this.</p><p>The six steps, the same for 2NF, 3NF and BCNF:</p><ol><li>Identify a problem functional dependency, one that violates the normal form you are targeting.</li><li>Remove the dependent attributes from the original relation.</li><li>Place the determinant in a new relation along with those dependent attributes. Loosely, the problem dependency becomes the new relation.</li><li>In the original relation, keep the primary key and the attributes fully dependent on all of it.</li><li>The determinant becomes the primary key of the new relation and <strong>stays in the original relation as a foreign key</strong>. This is the step that makes the decomposition lossless. Forget it and there is nothing to join on.</li><li>Repeat until every relation is at the desired normal form.</li></ol>"}, {"id": "p5-dependency-diagrams", "t": "Dependency diagrams", "h": "<p>A dependency diagram maps the FDs onto the list of attributes. For each FD, a line connects its attributes: plain connections mark the determinant, arrowheads mark the dependents. The practicum hands you one of these and asks for the 2NF and 3NF relations.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><div class=\"scroll\"><svg class=\"dia\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 236\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMin meet\" aria-label=\"Arrows below the row are the problem dependencies\" font-size=\"13\"><defs><marker id=\"dep-arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" marker-width=\"6\" marker-height=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" fill=\"var(--line-strong)\"></path></marker><marker id=\"dep-arrow-pd\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" marker-width=\"6\" marker-height=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" fill=\"var(--accent)\"></path></marker><marker id=\"dep-arrow-td\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" marker-width=\"6\" marker-height=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" fill=\"var(--key)\"></path></marker></defs><text x=\"24\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Arrows below the row are the problem dependencies</text><g><text x=\"24\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">The primary key (underlined) determines every other attribute</text><path d=\"M66 84H662\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></path><path d=\"M66 84V116\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></path><path d=\"M152 84V116\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></path><path d=\"M252 84V116\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></path><path d=\"M370 84V115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#dep-arrow)\"></path><path d=\"M474 84V115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#dep-arrow)\"></path><path d=\"M564 84V115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#dep-arrow)\"></path><path d=\"M662 84V115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#dep-arrow)\"></path></g><g><rect x=\"24\" y=\"116\" width=\"84\" height=\"36\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"108\" y=\"116\" width=\"88\" height=\"36\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"196\" y=\"116\" width=\"112\" height=\"36\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"308\" y=\"116\" width=\"124\" height=\"36\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"432\" y=\"116\" width=\"84\" height=\"36\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"516\" y=\"116\" width=\"96\" height=\"36\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"612\" y=\"116\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"87\" y2=\"142\" stroke=\"var(--fg)\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"179\" y2=\"142\" stroke=\"var(--fg)\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"211\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"293\" y2=\"142\" stroke=\"var(--fg)\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><text x=\"66\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">AutoID</text><text x=\"152\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">DriverID</text><text x=\"252\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">AccidentDate</text><text x=\"370\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">DriverLastname</text><text x=\"474\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">TypeID</text><text x=\"564\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">hasInjury</text><text x=\"662\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">TypeDesc</text></g><g><path d=\"M152 152V184H370V153\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dep-arrow-pd)\"></path><text x=\"261\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Partial dependency</text><text x=\"261\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">starts at part of the key: remove for 2NF</text></g><g><path d=\"M474 152V184H662V153\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--key)\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dep-arrow-td)\"></path><text x=\"568\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Transitive dependency</text><text x=\"568\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">non-key to non-key: remove for 3NF</text></g></svg></div><figcaption>dependency diagram: DeliveryVehicleAccidents, Fall 2023 practicum</figcaption></figure><p>How to read it:</p><ul><li>Primary key attributes are underlined. Here the key is composite: AutoID, DriverID, AccidentDate.</li><li>The line above shows the key determining every other attribute.</li><li>A line below that starts at <strong>part of the key</strong> is a partial dependency: DriverID → DriverLastname.</li><li>A line below that runs from <strong>one non-key attribute to another</strong> is a transitive dependency: TypeID → TypeDesc.</li></ul><p>Worked through, exactly as the instructor's solution does it:</p><p><strong>Start (1NF).</strong> <code class=\"rel\">DeliveryVehicleAccidents(<span class=\"pk\">AutoID</span>, <span class=\"pk\">DriverID</span>, <span class=\"pk\">AccidentDate</span>, DriverLastname, TypeID, hasInjury, TypeDesc)</code></p><p><strong>2NF: remove partial dependencies only.</strong> Move DriverID → DriverLastname to a new relation. DriverID stays in the original, where it is still part of the primary key and is now also a foreign key. Do not touch the transitive dependency yet.</p><ul><li><code class=\"rel\">DeliveryVehicleAccidents(<span class=\"pk\">AutoID</span>, <span class=\"pk fk\">DriverID</span>, <span class=\"pk\">AccidentDate</span>, TypeID, hasInjury, TypeDesc)</code></li><li><code class=\"rel\">Driver(<span class=\"pk\">DriverID</span>, DriverLastname)</code></li></ul><p><strong>3NF: start from the 2NF answer and remove transitive dependencies.</strong> Move TypeID → TypeDesc to a new relation. TypeID stays behind as a foreign key.</p><ul><li><code class=\"rel\">DeliveryVehicleAccidents(<span class=\"pk\">AutoID</span>, <span class=\"pk fk\">DriverID</span>, <span class=\"pk\">AccidentDate</span>, <span class=\"fk\">TypeID</span>, hasInjury)</code></li><li><code class=\"rel\">Driver(<span class=\"pk\">DriverID</span>, DriverLastname)</code></li><li><code class=\"rel\">AccidentType(<span class=\"pk\">TypeID</span>, TypeDesc)</code></li></ul><p>On paper, draw a referential integrity arrow from each foreign key to the primary key it refers to. The arrow is your proof that the decomposition is lossless.</p>"}, {"id": "p5-how-to-tell-what-normal-form-a-relation-is-in", "t": "How to tell what normal form a relation is in", "h": "<ol><li>List every non-trivial, minimal FD.</li><li>Mark the candidate keys: the determinants that determine everything else. Their attributes are the prime attributes.</li><li>Any multivalued attribute? Then it is not even 1NF.</li><li>Any non-key attribute determined by part of a composite primary key? Then it is 1NF only.</li><li>Any non-key attribute determined by another non-key attribute, passing the logical test? Then it is 2NF only.</li><li>Any determinant at all that is not a superkey? Then it is 3NF only.</li><li>Otherwise it is in BCNF.</li></ol><p>Stop at the first form that is violated.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>The FD X → A looks like</th><th>It is</th><th>Violates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>X is a candidate key or superkey</td><td>Fine</td><td>Nothing</td></tr><tr><td>X is part of the composite primary key, A is non-key</td><td>Partial dependency</td><td>2NF</td></tr><tr><td>X is non-key, A is non-key</td><td>Transitive dependency</td><td>3NF</td></tr><tr><td>X is not a superkey, A is prime</td><td>Non-superkey dependency</td><td>BCNF only</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "p5-one-relation-all-the-way-up-textbook-example", "t": "One relation, all the way up (textbook example)", "h": "<p><code class=\"rel\">Work(<span class=\"pk\">projName</span>, <span class=\"pk\">empId</span>, projMgr, hours, empName, budget, startDate, salary, empMgr, empDept, rating)</code></p><p>FDs, from the stated assumptions:</p><ul><li>projName → projMgr, budget, startDate</li><li>empId → empName, salary, empMgr, empDept</li><li>projName, empId → hours, rating</li><li>empDept → empMgr (each department has one manager)</li></ul><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Finding</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1NF</td><td>Every cell is single-valued</td><td>Already 1NF</td></tr><tr><td>2NF</td><td>Two partial dependencies: projName → ..., and empId → ...</td><td><code class=\"rel\">Proj(<span class=\"pk\">projName</span>, projMgr, budget, startDate)</code>; <code class=\"rel\">Emp(<span class=\"pk\">empId</span>, empName, salary, empMgr, empDept)</code>; <code class=\"rel\">Work1(<span class=\"pk fk\">projName</span>, <span class=\"pk fk\">empId</span>, hours, rating)</code></td></tr><tr><td>3NF</td><td>In Emp, empDept → empMgr is transitive</td><td><code class=\"rel\">Emp1(<span class=\"pk\">empId</span>, empName, salary, <span class=\"fk\">empDept</span>)</code>; <code class=\"rel\">Dept(<span class=\"pk\">empDept</span>, empMgr)</code></td></tr><tr><td>BCNF</td><td>In each relation the only determinant is the primary key</td><td>Already BCNF</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Note that Work1 must be kept even if it held nothing but its key: it is the connecting relation that lets Proj and Emp be joined back together.</p><p>Things that are not FDs in this example: projMgr → budget (a manager setting a budget is cause, not functional dependency), and empMgr → empDept (manager names are not unique).</p>"}, {"id": "p5-three-routes-to-a-normalized-design", "t": "Three routes to a normalized design", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Analysis (decomposition)</td><td>Top-down. Put every attribute in one <strong>universal relation</strong>, find the FDs, decompose.</td></tr><tr><td>Synthesis</td><td>Bottom-up. Start from attributes and FDs and group them into relations.</td></tr><tr><td>From an ER diagram</td><td>What designers actually do. Map the diagram to tables, which are usually already well normalized, then check each relation and fix any that fall short.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]};
